These checks can't go inside `db.write()`, because it takes raw SQL and has no request context. If you want a backstop for future routes that forget the filter, use Postgres row-level security: `write()` would take the account ID and run `SET LOCAL app.account_id` inside the transaction it already opens.

Even with this fix, any member of an account can soft-delete the whole account, because `req.user` has no role to check.

Put the check in `sessionUser` in `auth.js`. Every route already passes through it, so a deleted account's users get a 401 everywhere and no route has to remember the column:

```js
const rows = await db.query(
  "SELECT u.id, u.email, u.account_id FROM sessions s JOIN users u ON u.id = s.user_id JOIN accounts a ON a.id = u.account_id WHERE s.token = $1 AND s.expires_at > now() AND a.deleted_at IS NULL",
  [req.cookies && req.cookies.session]
);
```

This covers all routes only if they scope by `req.user.accountId`, which is the fix from the previous answer. Right now, a user from a live account can still update a deleted account by its ID.

The remaining question is whether deletion can be undone. If there's a restore window, this filter is enough, and a later job can hard-delete accounts past the window. If deletion should be final immediately, the delete route should also remove the account's sessions.

